output.text:
{"connection_id": "BC-051f9b2fbb217d3f8165", "ref": "Didache 1.1", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "way of life and way of death", "note": "The commentary's two forms of going are set against each other; the Didache opens by naming two ways and says their difference is great."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f7c886236c3e2897d74", "ref": "Didache 1:1", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two ways, of life and death", "note": "The Didache's opening contrast of the Way of Life and the Way of Death makes the commentary's two kinds of going into an early Christian ethical map."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ed7512a87e1318465e1", "ref": "Didache 5.1", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "way of death", "note": "The commentary presents dalal as a way whose destination is lost; the Didache specifies the way of death through the conduct that marks it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-604b18ed5977f4ea1790", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "perishable body raised imperishable", "note": "The commentary's earth-disappearance question concerns whether the dead can be made new; Paul describes what is sown perishable being raised imperishable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-577ef657f7b0da6ced4d", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.8.4", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "idols have no real existence", "note": "The commentary says the fabricated objects disappear from their adherents; Paul says an idol is nothing in the world, recasting their failure as the absence of a true divine power."}
{"connection_id": "BC-561916d18edcbd78c075", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.8.4", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "an idol has no real being", "note": "Paul's claim that an idol has no real existence supplies a Christian conceptual counterpart to the commentary's false, fabricated objects proving absent rather than dependable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-354cea0ab0cf70d42bb8", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "straying sheep returned to their shepherd", "note": "The readers were straying like sheep but have returned to the shepherd, a concise Christian movement from lost wandering to a responsible destination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-016bcfcc624e613abc1c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.1.9", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "turn from idols to serve the living God", "note": "The commentary says fabricated objects leave their worshippers; Paul describes converts turning from idols to serve the living and true God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8048b51b7aa0b83a45c4", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.4.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dead in Christ rise", "note": "The commentary's people lost in earth ask about new creation; Paul says the dead in Christ rise at the Lord's descent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dd2e866c51b0bd0a3c98", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.4.17", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "meet the Lord", "note": "The commentary's reply to disappearance is framed as meeting the Lord; Paul says the living and resurrected will be caught up to meet him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-637212bb251da0755413", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.14", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "raised and presented with believers", "note": "God raises believers with Jesus and presents them together; resurrection is explicitly a movement into God's presence rather than a terminal vanishing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7972691a316c8f6083e6", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.1.9", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blindness joined to forgetfulness", "note": "The commentary gives a secondary sense of dalal as something vanishing from memory; Peter links short-sighted blindness with forgetting the cleansing from former sins."}
{"connection_id": "BC-02f326e6456055c0f66f", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.2.15", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "forsaking the straight way and going astray", "note": "The false teachers abandon the straight way and go astray, directly setting a right route against wandering in the terms the commentary contrasts."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8265ea70ba5076cecfc0", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.2.19", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the Lord knows his own", "note": "The commentary says God neither loses nor forgets; Paul says the Lord knows those who are his, marking divine recognition as secure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ac364ecd6e2b1365998c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.16.9", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Macedonian vision redirects the journey", "note": "The commentary says a road has direction and destination; Paul's vision of a Macedonian asking for help redirects his travel toward a new destination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f40d5e73301511dc2fb6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.10", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Joseph delivered and made ruler", "note": "The commentary says Joseph's disappearance leads into the road of travelers; Stephen's retelling continues with God delivering Joseph and making him ruler in Egypt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-964343cd06178df23eee", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.9", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Joseph sold, yet God was with him", "note": "The commentary's Joseph vanishes from his brothers and passes into travelers' hands; Stephen retells the sale to Egypt while stressing that God was with Joseph."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ce25573966e9e7c05776", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.7.9", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Joseph sold into Egypt, yet God with him", "note": "Stephen's retelling retains the brothers' sale of Joseph but adds that God was with him, explicitly reading the apparent loss as a providentially accompanied passage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd7dbdbcf366375ae5ef", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.8.26", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Philip directed onto a desert road", "note": "The commentary describes a traveler on a route with a destination; an angel sends Philip onto the Jerusalem-to-Gaza road, where the encounter that follows leads to the eunuch's baptism."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bfa81fda8892a66d2de8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.19", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "confidence to enter the holy places", "note": "The verse names entry into God's holy presence as the believers' goal, the arrival required to read the next verse's new and living way as more than a road metaphor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7922abb648efc679057f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.20", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "new and living way through the veil", "note": "Jesus inaugurates a new and living way through the veil; the Christian image joins a specifically living route to access before God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d401063abbe13c017dad", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.29", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Red Sea crossed as dry land, Egyptians swallowed by it", "note": "Israel crosses the sea as dry ground while the Egyptians are swallowed when they attempt it, sharply contrasting water as a way of arrival with water as consuming loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2b2025d856bf4b08fdcc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.13", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "make straight paths so the lame may heal", "note": "The commentary requests a straight road; Hebrews urges straight paths for the feet so the impaired limb may be healed rather than further dislocated."}
{"connection_id": "BC-39261ffd4a6778fef5d3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.13", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "make straight paths for your feet", "note": "The exhortation to make straight paths for the feet seeks restoration rather than dislocation for the lame, a pastoral Christian use of straight-path imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f8bc173a8fd019ee7070", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.10", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "hearts go astray and do not know God's ways", "note": "Hebrews applies the wilderness generation's persistent going astray to its failure to know God's ways, directly coupling dalal-like error with an unrecognized divine route."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a8f1b8e4ca9f19bdbb35", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.3.11", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "exclusion from God's rest", "note": "The divine oath that the straying generation will not enter God's rest supplies the destination denied to those who have not known God's ways."}
{"connection_id": "BC-931b63079fd5e16b3c4c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.4.13", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "nothing hidden from God's sight", "note": "No creature is unseen before God and all are exposed to him, supplying the divine-sight side of the commentary's assertion that nobody can be absent from judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-760446a508211dc34274", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.10", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God is not unjust to forget work", "note": "Hebrews explicitly denies that God forgets the readers' work and love, a direct theological counterpart to the commentary's negation of divine loss and forgetting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c7149719655d56d951df", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.8.12", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Jeremiah's promise applied to the new covenant", "note": "The commentary's claim that God does not lose or forget meets Hebrews' quotation of Jeremiah that God will remember sins no more; Hebrews frames that promise as the new covenant established through Jesus."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4394f8cdcfd44f4045af", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.8.12", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "God will no longer remember sins", "note": "Hebrews uses divine non-remembrance as covenantal forgiveness of sins, not as failed recall; that deliberate sense of \"not remember\" usefully distinguishes it from the commentary's claim that God never loses knowledge or forgets."}

{"connection_id": "BC-16687c3497655368deb1", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.12.35", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walking in darkness without knowing where", "note": "The commentary uses dalal for one who goes where no one knows; Jesus warns that the one walking in darkness does not know where he is going."}
{"connection_id": "BC-747b1ba236ec9e05d115", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.12.35", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "walking in darkness without knowing where one goes", "note": "Jesus says that the person walking in darkness does not know where he is going, an exact formulation of movement whose destination has vanished from knowledge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-158bf77deae0520f48ea", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.14.5", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "unknown destination and unknown way", "note": "Thomas explicitly links not knowing Jesus' destination with not knowing the way, the very relation between route and arrival made central in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-770e4a174e5704697a5b", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.14.6", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jesus as way to the Father", "note": "Jesus identifies himself as the way by which one comes to the Father; it is a Christian construal of a road whose point is arrival before God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aa08fd6effade22b0957", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.28", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the dead hear and come out", "note": "The commentary's dead are hidden in earth; Jesus says all in the tombs will hear his voice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fa144d59a4cd3e24f2f3", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.28", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "all in tombs hear the summons", "note": "All who are in tombs hear the Son's voice; the verse denies that hidden burial places can make the dead unavailable to resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-68c1738be7ba8df11acd", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.29", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "resurrection to life or judgment", "note": "The commentary's question concerns the destination after bodily disappearance; John distinguishes those who come out to life from those who come out to judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fdfdba9a3d9842177c73", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.5.29", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "emergence from tombs for judgment", "note": "Those in tombs come out for life or judgment, supplying both the new emergence and the accountable end that the commentary joins to earth-loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1435eada95d28ee6b9e8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.10.20", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "names written in heaven", "note": "The commentary says the Lord does not lose or forget; Jesus tells the disciples to rejoice that their names are written in heaven."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71391b4e7cb1a196bf98", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.6", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no sparrow forgotten before God", "note": "The commentary says the Lord does not lose or forget; Jesus says not one sparrow is forgotten before God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-54156746fd3e21b2e88b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.6", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "no sparrow forgotten before God", "note": "Not one of five sparrows is forgotten before God; the small-scale saying gives a vivid instance of divine attention that does not lose what creatures lose from sight."}
{"connection_id": "BC-07cbd47c19e34600b195", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.15.4", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lost sheep found in the wilderness", "note": "The commentary uses dalal for someone whose whereabouts are unknown; Jesus' shepherd searches the wilderness until he finds the lost sheep."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0cca4dfa194cbad488c7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.24.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unrecognized traveler on the road", "note": "The commentary says a traveler can disappear from the observer's sight while continuing along a route; on the Emmaus road, the disciples walk with Jesus but their eyes are kept from recognizing him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3667170bcb403163e959", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.24.31", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "recognition and disappearance at the destination", "note": "The commentary joins leaving sight with eventual arrival; at Emmaus the travelers recognize Jesus at the meal and he disappears from their sight, after the road has brought them to understanding."}
{"connection_id": "BC-447a865b3579cf202da0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.3.4", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Isaiah's straight paths applied to John's mission", "note": "Luke reuses the wilderness voice and straight paths for John the Baptist's preparation of the Lord's advent, a Christian framing of the same directed-road image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-14574c38946d08beb43b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Mark.1.3", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Isaiah's road read as John's preparation", "note": "The commentary treats the straight path as a route of divine guidance; Mark applies Isaiah's wilderness-road language to John preparing Jesus' arrival, giving the road a Christian messianic reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-26a5a6a2ffcf3501f4fc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.12.36", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "account for every careless word", "note": "The commentary says nothing can be absent from the Day of Judgment; Jesus says people will answer for every careless word on that day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-309ff99dfd5874aec2a1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.15.14", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blind guides and the pit", "note": "The commentary places blindness beside a path that cannot be seen; Jesus' image has a blind guide and blind follower both falling into a pit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-64c3923fbf424dca6e0e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.15.14", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "blind guides and a pit", "note": "Jesus' saying has a blind guide and blind follower both fall into a pit, making the commentary's sightless route-seeking and its well/pit image meet in one contrary destination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5ae4c15f4f1ca67e0ee6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.32", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all nations gathered for judgment", "note": "The commentary says the day gathers people and no one is absent; Jesus depicts all nations gathered before the Son of Man and separated for judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6b59cd61953b208f42ba", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.13", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "broad road to destruction", "note": "The commentary distinguishes a route that reaches a destination from dalal as disappearance without one; Jesus places the many on a broad road ending in destruction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e06f673e4d8f62e920bb", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.13", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "broad way leading to destruction", "note": "The broad road leads to destruction, furnishing the adverse route and end alongside Matthew's life-leading way."}
{"connection_id": "BC-678313b4cf96d042a93a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "narrow way to life", "note": "The commentary's sirat is a traversed way with an arrival; this verse makes the difficult way lead to life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a1d167870a80aa2b4e66", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "restricted way leading to life", "note": "The restricted way leads to life and is found by few; it makes the endpoint of a path, rather than mere motion, decisive."}
{"connection_id": "BC-024832d54e5bb70f6c30", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.20.12", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the dead stand before the throne", "note": "The commentary says no one is absent from the Day of Judgment; Revelation pictures the dead, great and small, standing before the throne as books are opened."}
{"connection_id": "BC-988c08a522032115ffd0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.20.12", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "books opened for the dead's judgment", "note": "The dead stand before the throne and are judged from opened books, joining the commentary's book, non-forgetting, and no-one-absent motifs in one judgment scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-abc720657c63b62b68c8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.20.13", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "sea, Death, and Hades give up their dead", "note": "The sea, Death, and Hades yield their dead for judgment, a precise final-assize counter-image to persons or things apparently lost in earth or water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e948ec8a5a4992d20cd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.14.10", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all stand before God's judgment seat", "note": "The commentary denies anyone can be absent from judgment; Paul says all will stand before God's judgment seat."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bc2ed0e1054f727b1c40", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.14.12", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "each gives an account", "note": "The commentary says no one escapes the Day of Judgment; Paul specifies that each person will give an account to God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6435a033e3c0a0aaacb6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.2.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hidden things judged through Christ", "note": "The commentary denies that anyone or anything is missing from judgment; Paul says God judges people's secrets through Jesus Christ."}

{"connection_id": "BC-b0f53bb1f666ab54801c", "ref": "1 Enoch 51:1", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "earth and Sheol return what was entrusted", "note": "The earth and Sheol give back what was entrusted to them; this treats apparent disappearance into the underworld as custody before eschatological return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-69438ced394a0987f99e", "ref": "1QS III,13–IV,26", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ways of light and darkness", "note": "The commentary sets the way of those graced against the lost; the Community Rule divides people between ways of light and darkness with opposed ends, showing a Second Temple Jewish two-ways frame."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b4b933aac1c93f43ed51", "ref": "2 Baruch 50:2", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "earth restores the dead", "note": "The commentary's question is whether the body lost in earth can be created again; 2 Baruch says the earth will restore the dead it now receives."}
{"connection_id": "BC-167e90bf0e3c73838ecd", "ref": "2 Baruch 50:2", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "earth preserves and restores the dead", "note": "The earth receives the dead to preserve them and restores them as received, making burial a guarded holding rather than annihilation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c111a215ba86235cc289", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:9", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "raised to everlasting renewal", "note": "The commentary reports the objection that the dead lost in earth cannot be newly created; the martyr answers that the King of the universe will raise the dead to renewed life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-09702aa805847f708364", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:9", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "the King of the world raises the dead", "note": "The martyred brother says that the King of the world will raise those who die for the law to everlasting life, a direct Jewish rebuttal to death as final loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e7a4ee3c375b4318eea8", "ref": "4 Ezra 7:32", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "earth gives up those asleep in it", "note": "The commentary's dalal includes burial in earth; 4 Ezra pictures the earth restoring those who sleep in it at the judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7db41f642ccda292ea4a", "ref": "4 Ezra 7:32", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "earth and dust give up their sleepers", "note": "At judgment the earth gives up those asleep in it and dust those dwelling silently, an unusually close Jewish apocalyptic answer to people thought lost in earth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-59beb201742b34b0cfc6", "ref": "Bavli Berakhot 29b", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "traveler asks for safe guidance and arrival", "note": "The commentary's sirat is a traveler's road with an endpoint; the traveler's prayer asks God to lead the journey in peace and bring the traveler safely to the intended destination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c07a19896b8db337ad35", "ref": "Bavli Rosh Hashanah 16b", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "books opened for judgment", "note": "The commentary joins God's complete knowledge to the Day of Judgment; the rabbinic account of books opened on Rosh Hashanah turns remembrance into a judgment record."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a5ff856ed8438668f6ff", "ref": "Bavli Sanhedrin 90b", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "resurrection established from Torah", "note": "The commentary presents resurrection as the answer to bodies disappearing into earth; the Talmud's debate derives resurrection from Torah, showing how Jewish interpretation makes that answer scriptural."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5533aa62e16406c06753", "ref": "Berakhot 60b", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God takes and will restore the soul", "note": "The morning prayer says God will take the soul and restore it in the time to come, a liturgical Jewish expression of temporary loss followed by restoration."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1d060ce0c058f89e341c", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 84:16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "pit containing no water but danger", "note": "The commentary treats the pit as Joseph's hiding place before the caravan route; Genesis Rabbah reads its emptiness of water as leaving snakes and scorpions, heightening the apparent finality from which Joseph emerges."}
{"connection_id": "BC-093375d29650b5015230", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 2:1", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "deeds written in a book", "note": "The commentary says God's knowledge is preserved rather than lost; Avot teaches that all deeds are written in a book, an ethical interpretation of divine accounting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37a79fe2af2ae1282014", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 2:1", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "every deed written in a book", "note": "The maxim's seeing eye, hearing ear, and book recording all deeds make the commentary's claim that the Lord neither loses nor forgets a concrete ethical-accounting image."}
