{"connection_id": "BC-900c0b88b9204a97d4a0", "ref": "WLC:Jer.31.9", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "straight path without stumbling", "note": "In the return-from-exile procession God leads people beside water on a straight path where they will not stumble; its physical homeward route gives a destination to divinely guided movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37e3cc88468cbda62713", "ref": "WLC:Jer.50.6", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "lost sheep led astray by shepherds", "note": "Jeremiah calls the people lost sheep whose shepherds led them astray across mountains; its failed guides give the commentary's dalal a communal and directional scene."}

{"connection_id": "BC-dc8a6511b89ef4792a2f", "ref": "WLC:Jer.6.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ancient path and good way", "note": "The commentary identifies a path with a direction and destination; Jeremiah tells the people to seek the good way, walk in it, and find rest."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2c3d0b9b6096a6cdd5e0", "ref": "WLC:Job.12.24", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "leaders wander in a pathless waste", "note": "The commentary contrasts a guided road with directionless disappearance; Job says God makes the leaders of the people wander in a trackless waste."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1eda3337cdf8ead28097", "ref": "WLC:Job.12.24", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "wander in a pathless waste", "note": "God makes leaders wander in a trackless waste; its stated absence of a road concretizes dalal as a going whose direction cannot be found."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a0ae9beddc2f66ba0398", "ref": "WLC:Job.12.25", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "groping in darkness without light", "note": "The commentary's blinded people cannot see the path; Job pictures leaders groping in darkness without light and staggering like a drunk."}
{"connection_id": "BC-422f5cc29af892cdafa7", "ref": "WLC:Job.12.25", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "grope in darkness and stagger", "note": "Those made to grope without light stagger like the drunk, supplying a close bodily counterpart to the commentary's blind people who cannot see a path."}
{"connection_id": "BC-34f8afbf046cbc41e948", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.14", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "will the dead live again", "note": "The commentary presents the question of new creation after bodies disappear into earth; Job asks whether a dead person will live again, preserving the question in a different voice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1ebbe7d56415269a655c", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.15", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God calls and the dead answer", "note": "The commentary connects disappearance in earth with eventual meeting; Job imagines God calling and the dead answering, giving the hoped-for return a personal encounter."}
{"connection_id": "BC-536b0b12924256b844c0", "ref": "WLC:Job.34.21", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God sees every way", "note": "The commentary says no one can be absent from the Day of Judgment; Job says God's eyes are on human ways and steps."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c35f72418f70b0ee3bc5", "ref": "WLC:Job.34.21", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God sees every human way and step", "note": "God's eyes are upon a person's ways and see every step, directly answering the commentary's difference between a traveller leaving human sight and nothing being lost to divine knowledge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d9056a96c32e174f1b43", "ref": "WLC:Job.34.22", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no darkness to hide in", "note": "The commentary denies that anyone can vanish from judgment; Job says evildoers have no darkness or deep shadow where they can hide."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a1a46bc539e520ea295b", "ref": "WLC:Job.6.17", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "water vanishes from its place in heat", "note": "Job's seasonal streams are scorched and disappear from their place; this is a textual water-loss image beside the commentary's example of something consumed and lost in water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd2552760f89d5f98775", "ref": "WLC:Job.6.18", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "caravans leave their course and perish", "note": "The same wadi scene has caravan routes turn aside into waste and perish, bringing the commentary's linked images of vanishing water and travellers without a destination into one scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-010661db6c902e7e1d81", "ref": "WLC:Lam.3.54", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "waters over the head and the cry \"I am cut off\"", "note": "The lamenter describes water closing over his head and concludes that he is cut off, a close scriptural scene of disappearance in water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-377a8a93952497d84925", "ref": "WLC:Lam.3.55", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "calling God's name from the lowest pit", "note": "From the lowest pit the speaker calls on God by name, making the concealed pit a place from which relationship and appeal remain possible."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cc6a8e9769cfb54d61f5", "ref": "WLC:Lam.3.57", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "God comes near to the pit-bound caller", "note": "God draws near when the speaker calls and says not to fear; this supplies the response that turns the preceding pit-disappearance into an encounter rather than abandonment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5df16fa7feb7b89fc010", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "book of remembrance", "note": "The commentary says God's knowledge of past generations is kept in a book; Malachi describes a book of remembrance written before God for those who fear him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5c1728170c0f4eb2ad99", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "book of remembrance before God", "note": "A book of remembrance is written before God for those who fear him, closely paralleling the commentary's divine book in which past generations are neither lost nor forgotten."}
{"connection_id": "BC-75ef5b4606b2848673cb", "ref": "WLC:Num.16.32", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "earth opens its mouth and swallows Korah's company", "note": "The earth literally opens and swallows the rebels and their households, a forceful Torah scene for the commentary's paired images of swallowing and disappearance into ground."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dda178f3c784082a6512", "ref": "WLC:Num.16.33", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "earth closes over the living as they descend to Sheol", "note": "The swallowed group goes down alive to Sheol, the earth closes over them, and they perish from the assembly; it gives the earth-loss image its punitive, destinationless extreme."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fa60f801e0f1b5bcada8", "ref": "WLC:Prov.12.28", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "path of righteousness is life", "note": "Proverbs makes the outcome explicit: the path of righteousness is life and its track has no death, sharpening the commentary's claim that the difference between two goings lies in where they lead."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3dfed641e8c37de90b0a", "ref": "WLC:Prov.14.12", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "way that ends in death", "note": "The commentary says a path has an endpoint while dalal loses the destination; Proverbs warns that a way may seem right but its end is death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-98536db8e8a17e04bd4e", "ref": "WLC:Prov.14.12", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a seemingly right way ending in death", "note": "The proverb warns that a way which appears right can end in death, making destination rather than outward direction the test of a road."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a0bc98f9a4bf5591c38", "ref": "WLC:Prov.15.24", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "upward path of life versus Sheol below", "note": "For the wise, the path of life rises upward in order to turn away from Sheol below; its vertical destinations sharpen the commentary's distinction between a journey that arrives and one that is lost."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cb5fadf0eaea827468bb", "ref": "WLC:Prov.2.13", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "leave straight paths for darkness", "note": "The commentary contrasts the straight sirat with dalal; Proverbs depicts people leaving straight paths to walk in darkness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bfb236d04cfe9471791e", "ref": "WLC:Prov.2.15", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "crooked paths", "note": "The commentary contrasts a guided path with directionless loss; Proverbs describes those whose paths are crooked and ways devious."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bfad2c055a45c16c9373", "ref": "WLC:Prov.21.16", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "wandering from understanding to the assembly of the dead", "note": "Proverbs gives wandering from the way of understanding an explicit resting place among the dead, a grave outcome for directionless movement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-24dd5c25f8d79e16a59e", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.6", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "make paths straight", "note": "The commentary calls the requested sirat straight; Proverbs says trust in YHWH and he will make one's paths straight."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cc0cc00e80329e0a5522", "ref": "WLC:Prov.4.18", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteous path grows brighter", "note": "The commentary's sirat is a straight way with an arrival; Proverbs depicts the righteous path as light growing toward full day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bb487c8afce426634153", "ref": "WLC:Prov.4.19", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "way of the wicked is darkness", "note": "The commentary distinguishes an arriving path from losing one's way; Proverbs says the wicked walk in darkness and do not know what makes them stumble."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f17fb5b7d9bb6bd8afb7", "ref": "WLC:Prov.4.19", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "wicked way as darkness and unknown stumbling", "note": "The wicked walk in deep darkness and do not know over what they stumble, directly pairing loss of sight and loss of bearing as in the commentary's blind rush toward a road."}

{"connection_id": "BC-ff2b63ee2cc4194528ef", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.6", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "two ways and their ends", "note": "The commentary contrasts the guided path of the favored with loss; this verse says YHWH knows the righteous way while the wicked way perishes, giving the same moral contrast an explicit outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-56ab62ee41f28bdc4ae2", "ref": "WLC:Ps.105.17", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Joseph sent ahead", "note": "The commentary reads Joseph's pit and sale as a journey with an eventual destination; the psalm retells the story as God sending Joseph ahead of his family."}
{"connection_id": "BC-235be07f7a27ba5bf1d8", "ref": "WLC:Ps.107.4", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lost in wilderness without a city", "note": "The commentary says dalal can mean going where no one knows where the person went; these travelers wander in a desert way and cannot find a city to settle in."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f552939093d1a9bdcf25", "ref": "WLC:Ps.107.4", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "wanderers without a city", "note": "The travellers wander in a desert route and find no inhabited city, a concrete itinerary whose missing destination illuminates the commentary's person who goes away with no known arrival."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0cd7b6bde24c8f62345f", "ref": "WLC:Ps.107.7", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "straight way to an inhabited city", "note": "The commentary contrasts directionless loss with a route that arrives; God leads the lost travelers by a straight way to a city."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bd00b5e038b2c1f41832", "ref": "WLC:Ps.107.7", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "straight way to an inhabited city", "note": "After wilderness wandering, God leads the travellers by a straight way to a city where they can dwell; it gives the road the arrived-at end that the commentary says distinguishes sirat from disappearance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7318155c85be31be95de", "ref": "WLC:Ps.115.5", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "idols cannot speak or see", "note": "The commentary says the objects on which people relied disappear; the psalm describes their mouths and eyes as useless, exposing the absence of a responding power."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bce6a25692183fbac795", "ref": "WLC:Ps.115.8", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "trusters become like idols", "note": "The commentary says the fabrications leave their worshippers; the psalm adds that those who make and trust them become like them, sharing their incapacity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2506457a26a4f40bda60", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.105", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word as lamp for the path", "note": "The commentary's blind travelers cannot see the way; the psalm calls God's word a lamp to the feet and light to the path, supplying the light they lack."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff98b4a15a74b050f736", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.105", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "lamp for feet and light for path", "note": "The psalm supplies light specifically for feet and a path, directly reversing the commentary's scene of people unable to see where the road lies."}
{"connection_id": "BC-92f016bc18a140cdce42", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.176", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "lost sheep sought by its master", "note": "The speaker confesses, \"I have gone astray like a lost sheep,\" and asks to be sought; this turns dalal as unknown departure into a personal petition for recovery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e1f69ccf35e3aecbf253", "ref": "WLC:Ps.121.8", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "guarding the going out and coming in", "note": "The commentary pictures a traveler who leaves sight but has a destination; the psalm's close asks God to guard the worshipper's going out and coming in continually."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a9913940d9dc0fbd1ac9", "ref": "WLC:Ps.139.13", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "darkness does not conceal", "note": "The commentary says no one is absent from judgment; the psalm says night is as bright as day to God, so darkness cannot hide a person."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c3598e08d6210c25817c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.139.8", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no place beyond God's presence", "note": "The commentary says people cannot disappear from the judgment day; the psalm asks where one could flee from God's spirit or presence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2cceca8f77d04d890575", "ref": "WLC:Ps.143.11", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lead on level ground", "note": "The commentary joins guidance with a straight road; the psalm asks God's good spirit to lead the speaker on level ground."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37bf9b936a3dee69ce36", "ref": "WLC:Ps.143.9", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "way to go", "note": "The commentary's sirat is a route toward an arrival; the psalmist asks God to make known the way he should go."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f253b40e3e717d87b1fd", "ref": "WLC:Ps.16.11", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "path of life in God's presence", "note": "The commentary says the sirat goes somewhere; the psalmist says God makes known the path of life, whose destination is joy in God's presence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4550d10d758a7379a105", "ref": "WLC:Ps.23.4", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "paths of righteousness", "note": "The commentary identifies the sirat as a directed road; the shepherd leads the speaker in paths of righteousness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-45ec7f64de46de63542a", "ref": "WLC:Ps.25.5", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "show me your ways", "note": "The commentary asks to be guided along a path; the psalmist asks God to make his ways known."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ee641dbd38a8eaa04dc5", "ref": "WLC:Ps.25.6", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lead me in truth", "note": "The commentary's path is a route under divine direction; the psalmist asks God to lead and teach him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6aa67712e26087df712e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.56.9", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tears kept and recorded", "note": "The commentary says nothing is lost from God's knowledge; the psalm asks God to keep the speaker's tears in a bottle and record them in a book."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d3d5430f0fb27dfbb96f", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 14:11", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "idols become stumbling blocks", "note": "The commentary says fabricated things disappear from their makers; Wisdom says human inventions become abominations and traps for souls, spelling out the spiritual harm of that reliance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-801499b2cbffd9cab288", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 5:6", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "strayed from the way of truth", "note": "The commentary contrasts a way that reaches its destination with dalal as losing the way; at judgment Wisdom's speakers confess that they strayed from the way of truth and lacked righteousness's light."}
{"connection_id": "BC-94118d8e4d51e0a519b9", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 5:7", "target": "sec12", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "trackless desert and unknown way", "note": "The commentary says the lost person has gone where no one knows; Wisdom's speakers say they journeyed through trackless deserts and did not know the Lord's way."}

