+{"connection_id":"BC-604b18ed5977f4ea1790","ref":"SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42","status":"rejected","reason":"The verse contrasts perishable and imperishable resurrection bodies but says nothing of burial as being lost in earth; Daniel 12:2 supplies the closer image.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-577ef657f7b0da6ced4d","ref":"SBLGNT:1Cor.8.4","status":"rejected","reason":"Paul calls an idol nothing in the world while discussing idol food; the verse does not picture fabricated powers vanishing at judgment.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Cor.8.4"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-561916d18edcbd78c075","ref":"SBLGNT:1Cor.8.4","status":"rejected","reason":"An idol's lack of divine being is a different assertion from the disappearance of imagined partners in the judgment scene.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Cor.8.4"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-354cea0ab0cf70d42bb8","ref":"SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25","status":"accepted","reason":"Wandering sheep return to their shepherd; the recovery motif is expressed with Luke 15:4 in the annotation.","paragraphs":[59],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.15.4","SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-016bcfcc624e613abc1c","ref":"SBLGNT:1Thess.1.9","status":"rejected","reason":"The converts turn away from idols; the idols themselves do not disappear, and no gathering or judgment is depicted.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Thess.1.9"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-8048b51b7aa0b83a45c4","ref":"SBLGNT:1Thess.4.16","status":"rejected","reason":"Christ's dead rise, but the verse does not connect their burial in earth to an apparent loss; Daniel 12:2 is closer to that image.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Thess.4.16"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-dd2e866c51b0bd0a3c98","ref":"SBLGNT:1Thess.4.17","status":"rejected","reason":"Meeting the Lord follows the prior resurrection verse, but this sentence concerns those remaining alive and says nothing of being lost in earth.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:1Thess.4.17"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-637212bb251da0755413","ref":"SBLGNT:2Cor.4.14","status":"rejected","reason":"Paul expects believers to be raised with Jesus, without the earth-disappearance image or the unknown-route distinction developed here.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:2Cor.4.14"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-7972691a316c8f6083e6","ref":"SBLGNT:2Pet.1.9","status":"rejected","reason":"Its forgetfulness concerns a person's former cleansing, not an object slipping from memory or God's knowledge of the dead.","paragraphs":[59],"evidence":["SBLGNT:2Pet.1.9"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-02f326e6456055c0f66f","ref":"SBLGNT:2Pet.2.15","status":"rejected","reason":"False teachers leave a straight way for Balaam's way, but the verse gives no unknown destination or disappearance; the proposed point repeats the basic way/straying contrast.","paragraphs":[60],"evidence":["SBLGNT:2Pet.2.15"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-8265ea70ba5076cecfc0","ref":"SBLGNT:2Tim.2.19","status":"rejected","reason":"The seal says the Lord knows his own; it does not address forgetting generations or losing a person's trace.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:2Tim.2.19"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ac364ecd6e2b1365998c","ref":"SBLGNT:Acts.16.9","status":"rejected","reason":"A vision asks Paul to cross to Macedonia; this is a specified journey, with no disappearance, wandering, or contrast of ends.","paragraphs":[60],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.16.9"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-f40d5e73301511dc2fb6","ref":"SBLGNT:Acts.7.10","status":"accepted","reason":"Stephen follows Joseph's sale with divine rescue and Egyptian leadership, making a Christian retelling of the apparent loss.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.7.9","SBLGNT:Acts.7.10"],"annotations":["S001-INC-YGL-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-964343cd06178df23eee","ref":"SBLGNT:Acts.7.9","status":"accepted","reason":"The verse explicitly joins Joseph's sale to Egypt with God's presence beside him, the annotation's distinct addition.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.7.9","SBLGNT:Acts.7.10"],"annotations":["S001-INC-YGL-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ce25573966e9e7c05776","ref":"SBLGNT:Acts.7.9","status":"accepted","reason":"Stephen's retelling interprets Joseph's removal as an event during which God remained with him.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.7.9","SBLGNT:Acts.7.10"],"annotations":["S001-INC-YGL-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-fd7dbdbcf366375ae5ef","ref":"SBLGNT:Acts.8.26","status":"rejected","reason":"The angel gives Philip a geographical road to Gaza; the verse itself has no lost traveler or destination uncertainty.","paragraphs":[60],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Acts.8.26"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-bfa81fda8892a66d2de8","ref":"SBLGNT:Heb.10.19","status":"rejected","reason":"Entry to the sanctuary is the next verse's Christian goal, but 10:19 alone supplies no path or contrast with losing one's way.","paragraphs":[60],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Heb.10.19"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-7922abb648efc679057f","ref":"SBLGNT:Heb.10.20","status":"rejected","reason":"The living way through Jesus' flesh belongs to a sanctuary-access argument; no disappearance or lost destination is in this verse.","paragraphs":[60],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Heb.10.20"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d401063abbe13c017dad","ref":"SBLGNT:Heb.11.29","status":"accepted","reason":"The Greek explicitly sets passage through the sea as a way for Israel against the Egyptians being swallowed there.","paragraphs":[59],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Heb.11.29"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-001"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-2b2025d856bf4b08fdcc","ref":"SBLGNT:Heb.12.13","status":"rejected","reason":"Straight tracks serve healing of a lame member; the pastoral concern does not add the unknown destination or disappearance distinction here.","paragraphs":[60],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Heb.12.13"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-39261ffd4a6778fef5d3","ref":"SBLGNT:Heb.12.13","status":"rejected","reason":"The Greek says to make straight tracks for feet so lameness heals, a restorative image already beyond this paragraph's two kinds of going.","paragraphs":[60],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Heb.12.13"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-f8bc173a8fd019ee7070","ref":"SBLGNT:Heb.3.10","status":"rejected","reason":"The wilderness generation errs in heart and fails to know God's ways; it restates moral waywardness without the more precise lost destination of Psalm 107:4-7.","paragraphs":[60],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Heb.3.10"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a8f1b8e4ca9f19bdbb35","ref":"SBLGNT:Heb.3.11","status":"rejected","reason":"The denied entry to divine rest depends on the prior wandering statement; this verse alone names exclusion, not the road or an unknown arrival.","paragraphs":[60],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Heb.3.11"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-931b63079fd5e16b3c4c","ref":"SBLGNT:Heb.4.13","status":"rejected","reason":"Nothing being hidden before God describes exposure to divine sight, but the verse does not place that claim at resurrection or a judgment day.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Heb.4.13"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-760446a508211dc34274","ref":"SBLGNT:Heb.6.10","status":"rejected","reason":"God's not forgetting refers specifically to the readers' work and love; it does not concern Moses' lost history or bodies in earth.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Heb.6.10"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c7149719655d56d951df","ref":"SBLGNT:Heb.8.12","status":"rejected","reason":"The quotation promises not to remember forgiven sins; that is covenantal pardon, not failed divine recall, so it cannot oppose Moses' claim.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Heb.8.12"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-4394f8cdcfd44f4045af","ref":"SBLGNT:Heb.8.12","status":"rejected","reason":"Non-remembrance follows mercy toward sins, a deliberate forgiveness idiom rather than cognitive forgetting or loss.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Heb.8.12"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-16687c3497655368deb1","ref":"SBLGNT:John.12.35","status":"accepted","reason":"The walker in darkness explicitly does not know where he is going; the annotation joins this to John's destination/way exchange.","paragraphs":[60],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.12.35","SBLGNT:John.14.5","SBLGNT:John.14.6"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-747b1ba236ec9e05d115","ref":"SBLGNT:John.12.35","status":"accepted","reason":"The Greek wording directly couples walking in darkness to ignorance of destination, the point stated in the annotation.","paragraphs":[60],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.12.35","SBLGNT:John.14.5","SBLGNT:John.14.6"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-158bf77deae0520f48ea","ref":"SBLGNT:John.14.5","status":"accepted","reason":"Thomas explicitly connects not knowing Jesus' destination with not knowing the way.","paragraphs":[60],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.12.35","SBLGNT:John.14.5","SBLGNT:John.14.6"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-770e4a174e5704697a5b","ref":"SBLGNT:John.14.6","status":"accepted","reason":"Jesus identifies himself as the way of coming to the Father; the annotation treats this as John's specifically Christian answer.","paragraphs":[60],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.12.35","SBLGNT:John.14.5","SBLGNT:John.14.6"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-aa08fd6effade22b0957","ref":"SBLGNT:John.5.28","status":"rejected","reason":"Those in tombs hear a voice, but the verse neither mentions being lost in earth nor the destination; Daniel 12:2 gives the closer dust-and-waking scene.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.5.28"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-fa144d59a4cd3e24f2f3","ref":"SBLGNT:John.5.28","status":"rejected","reason":"Hearing from tombs is a valid resurrection motif, but the candidate's claim that burial makes the dead unavailable is an inference not stated here.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.5.28"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-68c1738be7ba8df11acd","ref":"SBLGNT:John.5.29","status":"rejected","reason":"The verse divides resurrection into life and judgment but has no earth-loss or disappearance image; Daniel 12:2 states that contrast with dust.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.5.29"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-fdfdba9a3d9842177c73","ref":"SBLGNT:John.5.29","status":"rejected","reason":"Emergence from tombs is only in the preceding verse; 5:29 itself names two resurrection outcomes, less close than the selected dust text.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:John.5.29"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-1435eada95d28ee6b9e8","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.10.20","status":"rejected","reason":"Names written in heaven concern disciples' assurance, not God's retention of past generations' history or judgment of the absent.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.10.20"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-71391b4e7cb1a196bf98","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.12.6","status":"rejected","reason":"God's care for sparrows is explicit, but its pastoral scale does not address an apparently lost person at judgment.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.12.6"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-54156746fd3e21b2e88b","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.12.6","status":"rejected","reason":"No sparrow is forgotten before God, yet the saying concerns care for living creatures rather than the historical record or burial image of this paragraph.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.12.6"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-07cbd47c19e34600b195","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.15.4","status":"accepted","reason":"The shepherd searches until finding the lost sheep, turning disappearance into recoverable absence.","paragraphs":[59],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.15.4","SBLGNT:1Pet.2.25"],"annotations":["S001-INC-MTF-002"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-0cca4dfa194cbad488c7","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.24.16","status":"rejected","reason":"The disciples fail to recognize a fellow traveler; he has not disappeared from view, so the proposed analogy misstates the scene.","paragraphs":[59],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.24.16"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-3667170bcb403163e959","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.24.31","status":"rejected","reason":"Jesus becomes unseen after the meal; this sudden disappearance is not the road carrying a traveler beyond an observer's view.","paragraphs":[59],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.24.31"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-447a865b3579cf202da0","ref":"SBLGNT:Luke.3.4","status":"rejected","reason":"Luke quotes Isaiah's prepared way for the Lord through John; the verse does not address travelers losing sight or unknown arrival.","paragraphs":[60],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Luke.3.4"],"annotations":[]}
